Article 8 The duties of judges:
(1) Participate in collegial panel adjudication or sole judge adjudication of criminal, civil, administrative actions, and state compensation cases according to law;
(2) Handle extradition, judicial assistance, and other cases according to law;
(3) Other duties as prescribed by law.
Judges shall be responsible for the cases they handle within the scope of their functions and powers.

Article 10 A judge shall perform the following obligations:
(1) Strictly abide by the Constitution and laws;
(2) Handle cases impartially and shall not bend the law for personal gain;
(3) Protect the litigation rights of parties and other participants in proceedings according to law;
(4) Safeguard national interests and social public interests, and protect the lawful rights and interests of individuals and organizations;
(5) Keep state secrets and secrets of adjudicative work, and maintain confidentiality of trade secrets and personal privacy learned in the performance of duties;
(6) Accept legal supervision and supervision by the people according to law;
(7) Enhance the concept of rule of law among the whole people and promote the construction of a law-based society by handling cases according to law and explaining the law through cases;
(8) Other obligations as prescribed by law.

Article 11 A judge shall enjoy the following rights:
(1) The powers and working conditions necessary for performing the duties of a judge;
(2) Not to be transferred, removed from office, demoted, dismissed, or disciplined except for statutory reasons and through statutory procedures;
(3) Occupational protection and welfare benefits to which a judge is entitled when performing duties;
(4) Personal, property, and residential security protected by law;
(5) To file a petition or accusation;
(6) Other rights as prescribed by law.

Article 12 The following conditions shall be met to serve as a judge:
(1) Have the nationality of the People's Republic of China;
(2) Support the Constitution of the People's Republic of China, support the leadership of the Communist Party of China and the socialist system;
(3) Have good political, professional, and moral character;
(4) Have the physical conditions to perform duties normally;
(5) Have a bachelor's degree in law from a regular institution of higher education and obtain a bachelor's degree or above; or a bachelor's degree or above from a regular institution of higher education in a non-law field and obtain a master's degree in law or a master's degree or above in law; or a bachelor's degree or above from a regular institution of higher education in a non-law field and obtain a corresponding other degree, and have legal professional knowledge;
(6) Have engaged in legal work for at least five years. For those who have obtained a master's degree in law or a doctoral degree in law, the required years of legal work may be reduced to four or three years respectively;
(7) A first-instance judge shall obtain legal professional qualifications by passing the national unified legal professional qualification examination.
Where it is truly difficult to apply the educational requirements specified in item (5) of the preceding paragraph, upon examination and confirmation by the Supreme People's Court, the educational requirements for serving as a judge may be relaxed to graduation from a regular institution of higher education within a certain period.

Article 13 The following persons shall not serve as judges:
(1) Those who have received criminal punishment for crimes;
(2) Those who have been dismissed from public office;
(3) Those whose lawyer or notary practice certificates have been revoked or who have been removed from the arbitration committee;
(4) Those with other circumstances as prescribed by law.

Article 20 A judge shall be proposed for removal from the position of judge according to law under any of the following circumstances:
(1) Losing the nationality of the People's Republic of China;
(2) Being transferred out of the People's Court where they serve;
(3) Position change does not require retention of the position of judge, or the person applies for removal of the position of judge and obtains approval;
(4) Being assessed as incompetent for the position of judge;
(5) Being unable to perform duties for a long time due to health reasons;
(6) Retiring;
(7) Resigning or being subject to dismissal according to law;
(8) Being no longer suitable for continued service due to disciplinary or illegal acts.

Article 23 Where judges are related by marriage, by direct blood line, by collateral blood line within three generations, or by close affinity, they shall not concurrently hold the following positions:
(1) President, Vice President, members of the Adjudication Committee, Division Chief, or Deputy Division Chief of the same People's Court;
(2) President, Vice President, and Judge of the same People's Court;
(3) Division Chief, Deputy Division Chief, and Judge of the same adjudication division;
(4) President or Vice President of two adjacent levels of People's Courts.

Article 24 Where the spouse, parents, or children of a judge fall under any of the following circumstances, the judge shall implement a practice of recusal:
(1) serving as a partner or founder of a law firm within the jurisdiction of the People's Court where the judge serves;
(2) acting as litigation agent or defender in the capacity of a lawyer within the jurisdiction of the People's Court where the judge serves, or providing other paid legal services to the parties in a litigation case.

Article 45 A judge who has any of the following performances shall be given a reward:
(1) Adjudicating impartially with remarkable achievements;
(2) Achieving outstanding results in summarizing adjudication practice experience, which has guiding significance for adjudication work;
(3) Making remarkable achievements and contributions in handling major cases, dealing with emergencies, and undertaking special important tasks;
(4) Proposing reform suggestions for adjudication work that are adopted and produce significant results;
(5) Proposing judicial suggestions that are adopted, or conducting legal publicity and education, guiding mediation organizations to mediate various disputes, with significant results;
(6) Having other meritorious deeds.
The reward for judges shall be handled in accordance with relevant provisions.

Article 46 A judge who has any of the following acts shall be given a sanction; if a crime is constituted, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law:
(1) Committing bribery, engaging in favoritism and malpractice, or perverting the law in adjudication;
(2) Concealing, forging, altering, or intentionally destroying evidence or case materials;
(3) Divulging State secrets, secrets of adjudication work, trade secrets, or personal privacy;
(4) Intentionally handling cases in violation of laws and regulations;
(5) Causing erroneous adjudication results and serious consequences due to gross negligence;
(6) Delaying case handling and causing work delays;
(7) Using authority to seek personal gain for oneself or others;
(8) Accepting benefits from parties or their litigation agents, or meeting with parties or their litigation agents in violation of relevant provisions;
(9) Engaging in or participating in for-profit activities in violation of relevant provisions, or holding concurrent positions in enterprises or other for-profit organizations;
(10) Having other violations of discipline or law.
The sanction of judges shall be handled in accordance with relevant provisions.

Article 53 Except under the following circumstances, a judge shall not be transferred from his or her adjudication position:
(1) Where job avoidance is required according to regulations;
(2) Where job rotation is implemented according to regulations;
(3) Where work adjustment is necessary due to institutional adjustment, dissolution, merger, or reduction of staffing;
(4) Where the judge is unsuitable to work in the adjudication position due to violation of discipline or law;
(5) Other circumstances prescribed by law.
